  "last_message": "Added `logout(member_id)` to `larder/sessions.py:41-43`, and all 5 tests pass (3 new).\n\nHow it works: `verify()` already derives the session's last-renewal time from `expires - EXPIRY_MINUTES`, so `logout()` just records when a member was logged out and `verify()` rejects any cookie renewed at or before that moment \u2014 no cookie format change needed.\n\n**Important caveat, flagged in the docs too:** per `docs/adr/0001-keep-sessions-in-signed-cookies.md`, larder deliberately has no server-side session store (an earlier Postgres-backed store grew unbounded and caused an outage). This `logout()` is in-process, so it only revokes the session on the server a volunteer calls it from \u2014 with two servers behind the load balancer, the stolen phone could still be accepted by the *other* server until its own 30-minute expiry. I updated the ADR's Consequences and `docs/features/sessions.md` to state this plainly, and noted that rotating `LARDER_SESSION_KEY` is the only way to guarantee logout across both servers immediately (at the cost of logging everyone out). I'd flag this to whoever handles the stolen-phone case: call `logout()` *and* consider the key rotation if both servers must be covered immediately.",
